WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.120 --> 00:00:01.680
- When I first started working

00:00:01.680 --> 00:00:05.460
as a consultant and communication
coach over 20 years ago,

00:00:05.460 --> 00:00:08.707
there was a sign on the wall
of a meeting room that said

00:00:08.707 --> 00:00:09.870
"No leader?

00:00:09.870 --> 00:00:10.703
No agenda?

00:00:10.703 --> 00:00:11.730
No time limit?

00:00:11.730 --> 00:00:13.560
No meeting!"

00:00:13.560 --> 00:00:14.393
In this video,

00:00:14.393 --> 00:00:17.490
you'll learn five tips for how
to run an effective meeting.

00:00:17.490 --> 00:00:20.070
It then certainly includes
elements on that sign,

00:00:20.070 --> 00:00:21.720
but other details as well,

00:00:21.720 --> 00:00:23.400
including a bonus tip at the end

00:00:23.400 --> 00:00:25.920
that will really elevate your meetings.

00:00:25.920 --> 00:00:27.000
And we're going to consider

00:00:27.000 --> 00:00:29.850
some of these prerequisite practices

00:00:29.850 --> 00:00:31.590
of running an effective meeting.

00:00:31.590 --> 00:00:33.630
That means practicing
some of these elements

00:00:33.630 --> 00:00:35.940
consistently will not guarantee

00:00:35.940 --> 00:00:37.950
that all your meetings will be productive.

00:00:37.950 --> 00:00:39.510
But these habits will prevent you

00:00:39.510 --> 00:00:42.840
from having unproductive
and ineffective meetings.

00:00:42.840 --> 00:00:44.850
And there are some more advanced tips

00:00:44.850 --> 00:00:45.900
that will elevate your meetings

00:00:45.900 --> 00:00:47.940
that we'll talk about
along the way as well.

00:00:47.940 --> 00:00:49.830
This is going to start out really basic,

00:00:49.830 --> 00:00:51.510
but I can almost guarantee

00:00:51.510 --> 00:00:54.330
that if you are currently
experiencing ineffective meetings,

00:00:54.330 --> 00:00:56.100
then your team is not doing

00:00:56.100 --> 00:00:58.590
one or more of these five practices,

00:00:58.590 --> 00:01:00.450
plus there's that bonus tip at the end.

00:01:00.450 --> 00:01:02.490
So use this as a checklist.

00:01:02.490 --> 00:01:05.130
Number one, the meeting
must have a leader.

00:01:05.130 --> 00:01:06.540
This might seem obvious,

00:01:06.540 --> 00:01:08.160
but I've been to numerous meetings,

00:01:08.160 --> 00:01:09.630
especially committee meetings

00:01:09.630 --> 00:01:11.280
where I work over the years,

00:01:11.280 --> 00:01:13.140
where the group is meeting regularly

00:01:13.140 --> 00:01:16.440
but there's no one clear
leader who's driving it.

00:01:16.440 --> 00:01:18.540
Sometimes the actual leader can't be there

00:01:18.540 --> 00:01:20.490
but they want us to
meet without them anyway

00:01:20.490 --> 00:01:22.110
to see what we can get done.

00:01:22.110 --> 00:01:24.420
This can be very confusing for everybody.

00:01:24.420 --> 00:01:28.470
So your first step is to
identify the leader beforehand.

00:01:28.470 --> 00:01:30.000
And if you're watching this video,

00:01:30.000 --> 00:01:32.820
I'm going to assume
that you are the leader,

00:01:32.820 --> 00:01:34.290
and the rest of the tips will help you

00:01:34.290 --> 00:01:35.610
become more effective.

00:01:35.610 --> 00:01:38.520
Number two, you must
have a written agenda.

00:01:38.520 --> 00:01:41.430
I strongly believe that there
should be a written agenda

00:01:41.430 --> 00:01:45.060
that is sent out a day or
so before the actual meeting

00:01:45.060 --> 00:01:48.000
so that everybody knows
how to prepare beforehand

00:01:48.000 --> 00:01:50.850
and then can fully
participate once they arrive.

00:01:50.850 --> 00:01:54.360
At least send a bulleted email list

00:01:54.360 --> 00:01:56.430
of topics that you'll discuss.

00:01:56.430 --> 00:01:59.550
So to me, this is like having
a leader in the first place.

00:01:59.550 --> 00:02:01.470
It's an obvious prerequisite.

00:02:01.470 --> 00:02:02.610
But in my experience,

00:02:02.610 --> 00:02:06.540
no written agenda is almost
the norm for many meetings.

00:02:06.540 --> 00:02:07.830
I've attended those,

00:02:07.830 --> 00:02:11.070
and those meetings are
seldom productive in the end.

00:02:11.070 --> 00:02:11.903
A few years ago,

00:02:11.903 --> 00:02:13.830
I had to send multiple requests

00:02:13.830 --> 00:02:15.390
about a meeting I was invited to,

00:02:15.390 --> 00:02:18.600
but I didn't know why
I was asked to attend

00:02:18.600 --> 00:02:19.500
in the first place.

00:02:19.500 --> 00:02:21.450
I never received an answer

00:02:21.450 --> 00:02:23.250
about what the meeting
was about ahead of time,

00:02:23.250 --> 00:02:24.780
let alone a written agenda.

00:02:24.780 --> 00:02:28.050
Not surprisingly, the meeting
was a complete waste of time.

00:02:28.050 --> 00:02:30.780
There are lots of variations of agendas.

00:02:30.780 --> 00:02:32.640
No two are identical.

00:02:32.640 --> 00:02:35.490
And it really depends upon the
norms of your organization.

00:02:35.490 --> 00:02:36.900
But generally speaking,

00:02:36.900 --> 00:02:40.530
agendas fall into two overall categories.

00:02:40.530 --> 00:02:41.490
There are meeting agendas

00:02:41.490 --> 00:02:43.740
that focus on sharing information

00:02:43.740 --> 00:02:46.680
and other agendas that
focus on decision-making

00:02:46.680 --> 00:02:48.180
or problem-solving.

00:02:48.180 --> 00:02:50.400
In a typical information-driven meeting,

00:02:50.400 --> 00:02:52.320
the agenda will list topics,

00:02:52.320 --> 00:02:54.480
like announcements, status updates,

00:02:54.480 --> 00:02:56.070
various topics of discussion

00:02:56.070 --> 00:02:59.970
including categories like old
business and new business.

00:02:59.970 --> 00:03:02.550
These meetings just
keep everybody informed.

00:03:02.550 --> 00:03:03.930
This is often the type of meeting

00:03:03.930 --> 00:03:04.897
that people will say,

00:03:04.897 --> 00:03:07.140
"That could have just been an email

00:03:07.140 --> 00:03:08.730
instead of a whole meeting,"

00:03:08.730 --> 00:03:12.030
which could be the topic of a
whole another video entirely.

00:03:12.030 --> 00:03:12.863
Less frequently,

00:03:12.863 --> 00:03:14.700
the other type of agenda will emphasize

00:03:14.700 --> 00:03:17.220
the key decisions that need to be made

00:03:17.220 --> 00:03:20.520
or the problems that the
group is there to solve.

00:03:20.520 --> 00:03:23.490
A few years ago, I was the
chair of my academic department.

00:03:23.490 --> 00:03:24.660
I'm a professor.

00:03:24.660 --> 00:03:26.070
We had a few meetings in a row

00:03:26.070 --> 00:03:29.310
that focused on solving problems
and making some decisions

00:03:29.310 --> 00:03:32.640
around restructuring our
undergraduate curriculum.

00:03:32.640 --> 00:03:35.070
Problem-solving meetings
like this typically follow

00:03:35.070 --> 00:03:37.800
the traditional problem-solving steps,

00:03:37.800 --> 00:03:39.900
and you would see this
on the written agenda.

00:03:39.900 --> 00:03:41.790
First, we identified the problem,

00:03:41.790 --> 00:03:44.040
then we analyzed the
problem in more depth,

00:03:44.040 --> 00:03:46.410
then we generated possible solutions,

00:03:46.410 --> 00:03:48.510
identified our best solutions,

00:03:48.510 --> 00:03:51.690
and finally took steps to
implement our decision.

00:03:51.690 --> 00:03:53.760
This decision-making process took

00:03:53.760 --> 00:03:55.590
a few meetings to complete,

00:03:55.590 --> 00:03:57.540
but I've run other
meetings where we progress

00:03:57.540 --> 00:04:00.030
through the entire problem-solving steps

00:04:00.030 --> 00:04:01.200
in just one meeting.

00:04:01.200 --> 00:04:02.880
And there's nothing magic

00:04:02.880 --> 00:04:05.280
about either approach to an agenda.

00:04:05.280 --> 00:04:08.820
Just make sure you create an
agenda for the sake of clarity

00:04:08.820 --> 00:04:10.620
and distribute it early.

00:04:10.620 --> 00:04:12.030
Get it out to everybody in advance

00:04:12.030 --> 00:04:13.590
so they can prepare fully

00:04:13.590 --> 00:04:15.990
and then show up and participate fully.

00:04:15.990 --> 00:04:17.640
And that leads to our next tip.

00:04:17.640 --> 00:04:18.473
Number three,

00:04:18.473 --> 00:04:22.260
each person must know what
the leader expects of them

00:04:22.260 --> 00:04:24.390
to contribute to each meeting.

00:04:24.390 --> 00:04:25.320
I believe strongly

00:04:25.320 --> 00:04:28.260
that the group leader should
let each individual person know

00:04:28.260 --> 00:04:30.540
specifically how they should prepare

00:04:30.540 --> 00:04:33.420
to participate in any upcoming meeting.

00:04:33.420 --> 00:04:35.700
So in addition to sending out the agenda,

00:04:35.700 --> 00:04:38.010
I always write a quick email

00:04:38.010 --> 00:04:39.120
or have a quick conversation

00:04:39.120 --> 00:04:41.527
where I say to each specific person,

00:04:41.527 --> 00:04:44.130
"You'll notice that I've
listed you on the agenda

00:04:44.130 --> 00:04:46.640
for an update on Project Z.

00:04:46.640 --> 00:04:47.580
In the meeting,

00:04:47.580 --> 00:04:49.830
could you let us know
how things are going,

00:04:49.830 --> 00:04:52.680
mention any recent
accomplishments on Project Z,

00:04:52.680 --> 00:04:53.820
and what your next steps are,

00:04:53.820 --> 00:04:55.200
and provide a rough timeline

00:04:55.200 --> 00:04:58.350
for when you think this
phase will be completed?"

00:04:58.350 --> 00:05:00.510
If there are eight people on my team,

00:05:00.510 --> 00:05:02.940
I'll send up to eight separate emails

00:05:02.940 --> 00:05:04.230
letting each person know

00:05:04.230 --> 00:05:06.570
how I'd like them to
prepare to contribute.

00:05:06.570 --> 00:05:08.040
And it works great.

00:05:08.040 --> 00:05:09.990
When I prepare people like this,

00:05:09.990 --> 00:05:12.600
they know why they're
there in their first place.

00:05:12.600 --> 00:05:16.500
They bring their A game because
the expectations are clear.

00:05:16.500 --> 00:05:18.540
The more experienced
team members will need

00:05:18.540 --> 00:05:20.310
less guidance over time.

00:05:20.310 --> 00:05:22.080
But if there's a brand-new
person on the team,

00:05:22.080 --> 00:05:23.430
I also let them know

00:05:23.430 --> 00:05:25.530
that while I'm not
expecting them necessarily

00:05:25.530 --> 00:05:27.450
to prepare anything big,

00:05:27.450 --> 00:05:29.550
I'd like them to make a contribution

00:05:29.550 --> 00:05:31.650
and look for ways to add value,

00:05:31.650 --> 00:05:34.320
even when we are simply
discussing an issue.

00:05:34.320 --> 00:05:35.153
I'll say to them,

00:05:35.153 --> 00:05:37.770
"Be sure you, number one, ask
a question during the meeting.

00:05:37.770 --> 00:05:39.810
Number two, offer your point of view.

00:05:39.810 --> 00:05:43.290
And number three, build on
what other people are saying."

00:05:43.290 --> 00:05:44.940
Those are the three easiest ways

00:05:44.940 --> 00:05:47.910
to get a new person up
to speed and contributing

00:05:47.910 --> 00:05:50.220
as soon as they join your organization.

00:05:50.220 --> 00:05:53.220
Number four, the leader must
encourage and facilitate

00:05:53.220 --> 00:05:56.610
maximum participation
during the meeting itself.

00:05:56.610 --> 00:05:59.370
Nobody likes attending
meetings that are life lesson,

00:05:59.370 --> 00:06:01.050
nobody's contributing.

00:06:01.050 --> 00:06:02.970
So in addition to the previous tips,

00:06:02.970 --> 00:06:04.080
it's also a good practice

00:06:04.080 --> 00:06:06.030
to send any additional materials

00:06:06.030 --> 00:06:08.010
we'd like them to read ahead of time

00:06:08.010 --> 00:06:09.870
and let them know how to prep up

00:06:09.870 --> 00:06:11.820
for those specific discussions.

00:06:11.820 --> 00:06:15.210
But the key to encouraging
maximum participation is

00:06:15.210 --> 00:06:19.050
by preparing good discussion
questions ahead of time.

00:06:19.050 --> 00:06:20.520
In a meeting situation,

00:06:20.520 --> 00:06:22.290
the best way I've seen this done is,

00:06:22.290 --> 00:06:25.110
first, the leader provides
some sort of information,

00:06:25.110 --> 00:06:26.820
a stimulus, we'll call it.

00:06:26.820 --> 00:06:30.540
Second, the leader then asks
a clear prepared question

00:06:30.540 --> 00:06:32.190
to get a response.

00:06:32.190 --> 00:06:35.370
The stimulus might be a
report you send out earlier,

00:06:35.370 --> 00:06:37.620
maybe some feedback you heard recently.

00:06:37.620 --> 00:06:39.210
The stimulus could be anything.

00:06:39.210 --> 00:06:40.860
And here's how it looks in the moment.

00:06:40.860 --> 00:06:42.720
You pull up the report or feedback,

00:06:42.720 --> 00:06:45.030
or whatever information you're sharing,

00:06:45.030 --> 00:06:48.330
and you keep it up so the group
can see it the entire time.

00:06:48.330 --> 00:06:51.690
And then you ask one
clear question at a time.

00:06:51.690 --> 00:06:54.120
You might even write the
questions on the agenda.

00:06:54.120 --> 00:06:56.820
You could put one question
up at a time on a slide

00:06:56.820 --> 00:06:59.910
depending upon the norms
of your organization.

00:06:59.910 --> 00:07:01.620
The information in this example,

00:07:01.620 --> 00:07:02.910
a report, the feedback,

00:07:02.910 --> 00:07:05.670
plus a clear question will create

00:07:05.670 --> 00:07:08.370
the ideal conditions
for a good discussion.

00:07:08.370 --> 00:07:09.630
If you miss either of these,

00:07:09.630 --> 00:07:11.400
you'll probably not generate

00:07:11.400 --> 00:07:13.800
as helpful of a discussion
as you could have.

00:07:13.800 --> 00:07:15.570
Groups have the best discussions

00:07:15.570 --> 00:07:17.430
when the leader provides the stimulus

00:07:17.430 --> 00:07:20.100
and then asks them to respond.

00:07:20.100 --> 00:07:22.110
Number five, and then I'll
give you the bonus tip.

00:07:22.110 --> 00:07:25.350
You must have a clear time
limit for lots of reasons.

00:07:25.350 --> 00:07:28.860
A time limit will make the
group work more efficiently

00:07:28.860 --> 00:07:30.780
rather than waste time.

00:07:30.780 --> 00:07:31.890
The number one thing

00:07:31.890 --> 00:07:35.250
that makes people work more
efficiently in a group is

00:07:35.250 --> 00:07:37.200
when people notice the time.

00:07:37.200 --> 00:07:39.780
So at some point in every
meeting I've been in,

00:07:39.780 --> 00:07:40.613
somebody will say,

00:07:40.613 --> 00:07:42.720
"Oh, we only have 20 minutes left.

00:07:42.720 --> 00:07:44.100
Let's get moving."

00:07:44.100 --> 00:07:47.040
So use that sensibility to your advantage

00:07:47.040 --> 00:07:49.680
by signaling the time
limits once in a while.

00:07:49.680 --> 00:07:50.513
And at a minimum,

00:07:50.513 --> 00:07:52.260
a time limit will at least bring

00:07:52.260 --> 00:07:54.780
the ineffective meetings to a close

00:07:54.780 --> 00:07:57.360
and end everybody's misery.

00:07:57.360 --> 00:07:59.730
A time limit also puts
more pressure on the leader

00:07:59.730 --> 00:08:01.350
to be at his or her best.

00:08:01.350 --> 00:08:02.820
A time limit makes the leader

00:08:02.820 --> 00:08:05.490
manage the time more
effectively in the moment

00:08:05.490 --> 00:08:08.880
and move the conversation
forward when it's dragging.

00:08:08.880 --> 00:08:10.470
A time limit will encourage the leader

00:08:10.470 --> 00:08:13.200
to reign in and refocus members

00:08:13.200 --> 00:08:15.630
who tend to ramble and go on tangents.

00:08:15.630 --> 00:08:17.430
A time limit will encourage the leader

00:08:17.430 --> 00:08:21.150
to make sure the overall pacing
of the meeting flows well

00:08:21.150 --> 00:08:23.520
so the proper amount of time is devoted

00:08:23.520 --> 00:08:26.190
to the most important
topics of discussion.

00:08:26.190 --> 00:08:29.010
One of the positive pieces
of feedback I received

00:08:29.010 --> 00:08:31.500
on my annual review when I was the chair,

00:08:31.500 --> 00:08:33.390
that I was secretly the most proud of,

00:08:33.390 --> 00:08:35.430
was that the faculty believed

00:08:35.430 --> 00:08:39.090
that we never had a
wasted moment in meetings.

00:08:39.090 --> 00:08:41.370
Every minute was time well-spent.

00:08:41.370 --> 00:08:43.740
And now for the bonus tip,

00:08:43.740 --> 00:08:46.140
the tip is to leave more space

00:08:46.140 --> 00:08:48.870
for your team members
to talk to each other.

00:08:48.870 --> 00:08:51.720
Instead of a mindset of
"running" the meeting,

00:08:51.720 --> 00:08:54.960
see yourself as "facilitating" a meeting.

00:08:54.960 --> 00:08:56.070
Here's what I mean.

00:08:56.070 --> 00:08:58.590
The most common pattern
I've seen in meetings is

00:08:58.590 --> 00:09:02.640
where a team member will say
something or ask a question

00:09:02.640 --> 00:09:05.820
and the leader then is the
first person to respond.

00:09:05.820 --> 00:09:06.780
This creates a pattern

00:09:06.780 --> 00:09:08.310
where the leader is really having

00:09:08.310 --> 00:09:10.620
a bunch of one-on-one conversations,

00:09:10.620 --> 00:09:12.600
even though you're in a group.

00:09:12.600 --> 00:09:14.850
It looks like a straight
line of communication

00:09:14.850 --> 00:09:17.850
going back and forth between
the leader and each person.

00:09:17.850 --> 00:09:22.140
Meetings like this rarely
feel dynamic and energetic,

00:09:22.140 --> 00:09:23.010
and it short-circuits

00:09:23.010 --> 00:09:25.290
what could have been a good discussion.

00:09:25.290 --> 00:09:27.960
Instead, those kinds of
meetings lack creativity.

00:09:27.960 --> 00:09:29.820
They lack collaboration.

00:09:29.820 --> 00:09:31.620
In short, this doesn't take advantage

00:09:31.620 --> 00:09:33.300
of the power of the group,

00:09:33.300 --> 00:09:34.530
which is one of the key reasons

00:09:34.530 --> 00:09:36.120
to have a meeting in the first place.

00:09:36.120 --> 00:09:38.250
Instead of a back-and-forth line,

00:09:38.250 --> 00:09:41.940
visualize the communication
pattern like a star,

00:09:41.940 --> 00:09:43.650
like the kind of star you draw

00:09:43.650 --> 00:09:46.890
where the communication bounces
from one person to the next

00:09:46.890 --> 00:09:48.810
until it goes full circle.

00:09:48.810 --> 00:09:51.240
And you as the leader
can create this pattern.

00:09:51.240 --> 00:09:52.170
To start it,

00:09:52.170 --> 00:09:55.080
when you ask a question or
when somebody says something,

00:09:55.080 --> 00:09:57.750
resist the temptation to jump in.

00:09:57.750 --> 00:09:59.820
Leave more time and space

00:09:59.820 --> 00:10:02.010
for the other team members to respond.

00:10:02.010 --> 00:10:04.020
So you'd still listen carefully,

00:10:04.020 --> 00:10:07.290
use good non-verbals like
eye contact and nodding.

00:10:07.290 --> 00:10:11.370
Maybe you'd make a small verbal
utterance, like ah or um.

00:10:11.370 --> 00:10:14.610
You'd still affirm them
non-verbally, in other words.

00:10:14.610 --> 00:10:18.390
But instead of saying something,
articulating your idea,

00:10:18.390 --> 00:10:22.260
look around the room to
invite responses from others.

00:10:22.260 --> 00:10:25.290
You could even ask them,
"What do you all think?"

00:10:25.290 --> 00:10:27.390
The goal of this is to
get the team members

00:10:27.390 --> 00:10:29.040
talking to each other

00:10:29.040 --> 00:10:33.030
in ways that lead to a dynamic
and collaborative discussion.

00:10:33.030 --> 00:10:34.530
And keep asking questions

00:10:34.530 --> 00:10:37.710
to keep that conversational ball rolling.

00:10:37.710 --> 00:10:40.650
The conversation then becomes
one that you facilitate

00:10:40.650 --> 00:10:43.290
rather than run or manage.

00:10:43.290 --> 00:10:45.540
If you get the conversational
dynamics right,

00:10:45.540 --> 00:10:49.530
your meeting will be 10 times
more satisfying for everybody.

00:10:49.530 --> 00:10:50.970
Here's the expanded checklist

00:10:50.970 --> 00:10:54.030
based upon the sign that I saw on the wall

00:10:54.030 --> 00:10:55.500
and that consulting company I work for,

00:10:55.500 --> 00:10:57.600
plus the other tips we added today.

00:10:57.600 --> 00:10:59.580
Again, many of these are prerequisites.

00:10:59.580 --> 00:11:00.960
That means this doesn't ensure

00:11:00.960 --> 00:11:02.760
you'll have lots of amazing meetings,

00:11:02.760 --> 00:11:06.150
